WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.055
Graphite consists of stacked carbon sheets.

00:00:03.105 --> 00:00:08.525
The bonds in each sheet are strong, while the
attraction between sheets is much weaker.

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:13.005
That difference makes it possible to peel thin
flakes from graphite.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.590
Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov pioneered
experiments with graphene.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.310
Mechanical exfoliation uses adhesive tape to peel
thin flakes.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.650
Identifying a single layer is an essential part of
the experiment.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.525
A suspended sheet can curve out of plane with a
small bending energy.

00:00:32.575 --> 00:00:35.700
Pulling it in plane stretches the carbon bonds.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.840
This is how an atomically thin sheet can bend easily
and still resist stretching.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:48.165
Pause and explain why it is easier to separate
sheets in graphite than to pull a carbon sheet
apart.

00:00:48.215 --> 00:00:53.015
Then listen for the experimental choices in Andre
Geim’s Nobel lecture.
